    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `label_ticket` in `app/labeler.py`. It sends one request to `MODEL` with `max_tokens=5`, a system prompt telling the model to answer with exactly one word from `LABELS`, the ticket text as the user message, and temperature 0. It lower-cases and strips the reply, and returns `\"question\"` if the result isn't one of the labels.\n\n**Temperature 0 couldn't go in the usual place.** The pinned SDK, `anthropic==1.8.0`, removed the `temperature` argument; it doesn't appear anywhere in the installed package. So I pass it as `extra_body={\"temperature\": 0}`, which is the SDK's documented way to send it in 1.x. It's still sent with every request.\n\nThis only works because the current model, `claude-sonnet-4-6`, accepts temperature. Newer models (Opus 4.7 and later, Sonnet 5, Fable) reject it with a 400 error. If `MODEL` is ever changed to one of those, every call will fail, and there will be no temperature setting left to make the labeler deterministic.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes, but the only existing test checks that the labels are lower-case, so nothing tests `label_ticket` itself. I didn't send a live request, and my attempt to check the SDK's function signature by running Python was blocked by permissions. I confirmed the parameters by searching the installed SDK source instead.\n\n**Context7:** `CLAUDE.md` asks for a Context7 lookup before writing SDK calls, but it returned \"monthly quota exceeded\". I used the installed SDK source and the bundled Anthropic API reference instead."
  },
